Dictionaries really should give some kind of indication of how
common a word is with a given meaning.  Your usage of "inapt"
wasn't wrong as such, but "inept" is a MUCH more common
word.  (Most college-educated people in the English-speaking
world have personally used the word "inept" in a sentence
within the last year.  Outside this thread, I think I've seen the
word "inapt" used one other time, ever.)

Actually, come to think of it, I do have a lexicon that gives
frequency -- as a number, no less.  (The three most most common
words have frequencies of 19870, 9153, and 5595; it goes down a
bit from there and then starts to level out somewhat.)  It is,
however, a lexicon for a dead language, and they just took the
number of times the word is used in a particularly important
set of ancient texts and used that for the frequency.  Deciding
frequencies for a modern, living language would be rather more
subjective.


